window.BH_SEARCH = [{"href":"docs/adr-001-rate-limit-in-each-service.html","title":"ADR-001: Rate limit inside each service","kind":"decision","example":true,"sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Architecture decision record ADR-001: Rate limit inside each service Each service enforces its own per-client token bucket in process, so we can ship limits before launch without new infrastructure. We will embed a per-client token bucket in every service, keyed by API key, because launch is three weeks away and this needs no shared infrastructure."},{"id":"context","heading":"Context","text":"The public API launches on 2 December. Two design partners have scripted clients that retry aggressively, and nothing today stops one of them saturating a service. We have no gateway able to run custom logic, and no shared low-latency store in production."},{"id":"options","heading":"Options considered","text":"In-process limiter per service Ships with a library and a config file No new runtime dependency Limits multiply with replicas and services Central limiter service One accurate limit per client New service, new on-call, before launch"},{"id":"consequences","heading":"Consequences","text":"What gets easier Each team tunes limits for its own endpoints What gets harder A client's real limit is per-service limit × replicas × services , so it grows as we scale Limits drift between services"}]},{"href":"docs/adr-002-rate-limit-at-the-edge.html","title":"ADR-002: Rate limit at the edge gateway","kind":"decision","example":true,"sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Architecture decision record ADR-002: Rate limit at the edge gateway Enforce one per-client token bucket in the gateway, with state in Redis, so limits hold however many services and replicas run behind it. We will enforce client rate limits once, in the gateway, using a token bucket updated atomically in Redis, because per-service limits no longer bound what a client can send. Services keep only a coarse safety limit against runaway internal callers."},{"id":"context","heading":"Context","text":"Under the per-service limits of ADR-001, one client sent 9× its contracted rate during the September incident without hitting a single limit: their traffic was spread across 3 services with 3 replicas each. We now have a gateway that runs plugins and a managed Redis cluster with p99 under 2 ms in-region. The token bucket explainer covers the algorithm, and the design document covers the request path, the script and the canary results."},{"id":"options","heading":"Options considered","text":"Keep per-service limits No migration The limit still multiplies as we scale Gateway + Redis token bucket One accurate limit per client Rejections never reach services Redis on the request path: +1–2 ms, needs a fail-open policy CDN provider limiting Absorbs floods before our network Can't see API keys or weight endpoints"},{"id":"consequences","heading":"Consequences","text":"What gets easier Limits are contractual numbers that hold One place to tune, observe and explain a 429 What gets harder Redis becomes part of every request; if it's down we fail open and lean on service safety limits"}]},{"href":"docs/announcing-patchcov.html","title":"Announcing patchcov","kind":"explainer","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Announcement · Testing Announcing patchcov patchcov answers one question precisely: which lines did this change add that no test ran? It is a command-line tool that reads the coverage report you already produce, and a GitHub Action that turns its answer into a pull-request comment and a gate. Neither needs an account, a token or a server. Command line & Rust library rust-works/patchcov Install it, run patchcov diff , read the docs GitHub Action action-works/patchcov-action Comment and gate on every pull request patchcov scores the lines a change added , lists the untested ones as file:line , and exits non-zero when a gate fails. patchcov-action runs it on every pull request, with a baseline from the merge base. That output is meant for automation as much as for people. An AI agent can loop on it, \"add tests until the gate passes\", without reading a dashboard. The project-wide percentage is the wrong signal for a pull request. A change can leave a third of its new code untested and barely move the total."},{"id":"motivation","heading":"Why another coverage tool","text":"More and more code is written by agents in a loop: write, run the checks, read the result, fix, repeat. That loop is only as good as the signals it can read. Coverage tooling was built to give people a picture, and an agent needs an instruction. Three things kept getting in the way: The total hides the change. A 4,000-line project at 92% looks the same after a pull request that adds six lines and tests four. The new code's gap is real, but the total is too coarse to show it. Dashboards need interpreting. Trend graphs and coloured file trees are made for a person to look at. A script or an agent needs a number, a list of lines, and an exit code. A hosted service is a dependency of your merge button. If the coverage gate lives on someone else's server, an outage, an expired upload token or a rate limit blocks merges. Nothing can be checked before you push, either. patchcov is the smallest tool that fixes all three. It reads report files from disk, joins them to git diff , and prints a result that needs no human to interpret it. The mission, from the README: help projects that use agentic workflows keep their new code properly tested, with minimal friction. Predict: a project has 4,000 executable lines at 92.00%. A pull request adds 6 executable lines and tests 4 of them. How far does the project total move? From 92.00% to 91.96%, a move of −0.04 percentage points. (3,680 + 4) ÷ (4,000 + 6) = 91.96%. That is under the 0.05 pp tolerance patchcov uses for its headline delta, so the total renders as neutral ⚪. The patch coverage for the same change is 66.67% , with two lines named. Try it below."},{"id":"patch-coverage","heading":"Patch coverage, hands on","text":"Patch coverage is the share of the added executable lines that some test ran. Click a line's mark to decide whether a test covers it, and watch both numbers. 40 pub fn parse_header(line: &str) -> Result<Header, Error> { 41 let line = line.trim(); 42 + // A shebang is not a header. 43 + ✓ if line.starts_with(\"#!\") { 44 + ✓ return Ok(Header::Shebang); 45 + } 46 + ✓ let Some((key, value)) = line.split_once(':') else { 47 + ✗ return Err(Error::MissingColon); 48 + }; 49 + ✓ if key.is_empty() { 50 + ✗ return Err(Error::EmptyKey); 51 + } 52 Ok(Header::Field(key.into(), value.trim().into())) 53 } Lines 42, 45, 48 and 51 are added but not executable: a comment and closing braces never appear in a coverage report, so they are not counted. 66.67% Patch coverage (4/6 new lines) 91.96% Project total ⚪ −0.04 pp, neutral --fail-under-patch 80 Patch: 66.67% (4/6 new lines covered) Uncovered new lines: src/parser.rs:47, src/parser.rs:50 Error: patch coverage 66.67% is below the --fail-under-patch threshold of 80.00% exit 1 Loop until the gate passes Add one test Reset The total barely moves however many lines you leave untested; patch coverage swings by 16.67 points per line and names each gap. “Loop” plays the agent’s part: it writes a test for the first line patchcov lists, runs the gate again, and stops when it exits 0."},{"id":"how-it-works","heading":"How patchcov works","text":"patchcov is a join between two things you already have: a per-line coverage report, and the lines git says the change added. Notice that nothing leaves the machine: every input is a file or a git object, and every output is stdout or an exit code. Read the report you already have. lcov, llvm-cov JSON, Cobertura, JaCoCo XML and Go coverprofiles are detected from the content, so one command serves Rust, Go, Java, Kotlin, TypeScript, Python, C++ and more. Paths are made repo-relative by a fixed, configurable pipeline, with no guessing: a wrong guess would attribute coverage to the wrong file without a sign. Ask git what the change added. By default that is everything from the merge base with the default branch to HEAD , so lines that reached main while your branch was open are not counted against you. Score each added line. It is covered, uncovered, or not executable. Lines the report doesn't mention, such as comments and braces, are left out of the denominator. Optionally, compare with a baseline. A report from the merge base adds per-file deltas and indirect changes: lines whose coverage flipped without their text changing. These are scoped to the files the diff touched, because two separate test runs always differ a little elsewhere. Print, then exit. The markdown is a ready-made pull-request comment; JSON and YAML follow a documented schema. The exit code is 0 , or 1 when --fail-under-patch or --fail-under-lines fails, and 2 – 8 for a usage, report, marker, config, git or path problem. So a script can tell a failed gate from a broken run."},{"id":"agent-loop","heading":"Built for the agent loop","text":"Because the answer is a list of file:line and an exit code, an agent can act on it without a person in between. Agent Test run patchcov tests + coverage head.lcov diff --fail-under-patch 80 exit 1 · parser.rs:47, :50 write 2 tests tests + coverage diff --fail-under-patch 80 exit 0 The agent never reads a chart. Each round trip is a command, a list of lines, and a yes or no. The agent runs the test suite under any coverage tool that writes a supported report, such as cargo llvm-cov , go test -coverprofile , coverage.py or JaCoCo. patchcov exits 1 and names the gaps. With -o json , they arrive as uncovered_new_lines , and --error-format json puts the cause on stderr as an object too. The agent writes tests for exactly those lines and runs both steps again. The same command works on a laptop, in a sandbox and in CI, so the agent can pass the gate before it pushes."},{"id":"trustworthy","heading":"A signal you can trust","text":"A gate that cries wolf gets ignored, by people and agents alike. patchcov is quiet where coverage is noisy and loud where it is broken. 1 Quiet by construction Deltas are scoped to the files the diff touched. The headline ignores moves under 0.05 pp. Code that flaps between runners, such as a path gated on a CPU feature, can be wrapped in a tolerate marker, so a flap isn't reported as a regression. 2 Fails loudly An empty report, a failed shard, report paths that match no tracked file, or a malformed marker is an error with its own exit code, not a quietly lower number. 3 Silencing is visible Every marker needs a reason= , and every exclusion is listed in the comment. Settings live in .patchcov/config.yaml , in version control, so a reviewer can see what the gate does. // patchcov: coverage tolerate reason=\"AVX2 path; runners differ in CPU\" fn popcount_avx2(words: &[u64]) -> u64 { /* … */ } // patchcov: coverage end Go deeper: why tolerated lines still count in the patch A tolerate region masks deltas : each tolerated line is scored with its status in the baseline, so a flap reads as no change. The displayed percentages stay the real, measured values. An added line has no baseline counterpart, so it keeps its real status and stays in the patch-coverage denominator: new code should still be tested, even if it flaps later. To remove lines from every number, use ignore instead."},{"id":"action","heading":"patchcov-action: the same answer on every pull request","text":"The command answers the question; the action wires it into GitHub. It installs a cached patchcov binary, finds the right baseline, posts one sticky comment, and applies the gates last. Notice that the baseline is pinned to the pull request’s fork point, not to the tip of main , so the deltas belong to this pull request alone. Every push to main publishes a baseline: that commit's per-line report, as an artifact named coverage-baseline . On a pull request, produce the head report. In fat mode (the default) the action runs cargo-llvm-cov for a Rust workspace. In thin mode you bring a report from any language, and the action only diffs, comments and gates. Fetch the baseline for the merge base. If that commit has none, it tries up to 10 first-parent ancestors, nearest first, and says so in the comment. As a last resort in fat mode, it rebuilds coverage at the merge base in a git worktree. A miss is a warning: patch coverage never needs a baseline. Post one comment and keep it current. It has the patch coverage, the per-file deltas and the uncovered file:line list, and is updated in place on every push. Gate last. fail-under-patch and fail-under-lines run after the comment posts, so a failing pull request still gets its explanation. The action also handles the topologies real projects grow into: sharded test runs merged into one result, merge queues, and files a CI runner can't execute. jobs: coverage: runs-on: ubuntu-latest permissions: contents: read actions: read # download the merge base's baseline pull-requests: write # post the coverage comment steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v7 with: fetch-depth: 0 # full history, so the merge base resolves - uses: action-works/patchcov-action@v2 with: fail-under-patch: 80 - run: pytest --cov --cov-report=lcov:coverage.lcov - uses: action-works/patchcov-action@v2 with: run-coverage: false report: coverage.lcov fail-under-patch: 80 coverage: needs: shard # a failed shard fails this job steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v7 with: fetch-depth: 0 - uses: actions/download-artifact@v8 with: pattern: coverage-shard-* merge-multiple: true path: shards - uses: action-works/patchcov-action@v2 with: run-coverage: false shard-reports: shards/shard-*.lcov fail-under-patch: 80 cargo install patchcov --locked # or: cargo binstall patchcov cargo llvm-cov --lcov --output-path head.lcov patchcov diff --report head.lcov --fail-under-patch 80"},{"id":"when-to-choose","heading":"When to choose patchcov","text":"patchcov is a signal, not a dashboard. Pick it for the question it answers, and keep the other tools for the questions they answer. patchcov A command and an action that score the change, in your own CI. Answers \"what did this change leave untested, and where?\" Machine-readable output and distinct exit codes, ready for agents No service, account or upload token: the gate works whenever your CI does Runs the same locally, in a sandbox and in CI Built for noisy coverage and sharded runs No history, trend graphs or cross-repository views Line coverage; branch scoring is opt-in and limited to lcov and Cobertura A hosted coverage service You upload reports; the service keeps them and comments on pull requests. Coverage history and trends over months Dashboards and views across many repositories A third party between your tests and your merge button Reports leave your CI; tokens to manage The result is learned after pushing, not before A code-quality platform Coverage as one input to a wider quality gate. Static analysis, security scanning and coverage in one place Another server to run or to depend on Much more to set up than a coverage gate needs Choose by the question you need answered. If you need… Choose To know whether a pull request added untested code, and where patchcov A gate an agent or script can act on, in a loop patchcov A coverage gate that can't be blocked by someone else's outage patchcov One combined result from a sharded test run patchcov Coverage history, trend graphs, or views across repositories A hosted service, alongside patchcov Per-branch percentages, or JaCoCo and llvm-cov branch data Your coverage tool's own report Static analysis and security scanning as well A code-quality platform You don't have to pick one patchcov-action can upload to Codecov as well ( codecov: true ), so the history keeps flowing to a dashboard while the gate stays in your own CI."},{"id":"limits","heading":"What it doesn't tell you","text":"Covered is not tested patchcov measures that a line ran , not that a test asserted anything about it. Treat it as a regression signal, and pair it with review, or with mutation testing, for confidence in the tests themselves. Measure the report at the revision you diff. A report from older code has line numbers that no longer match the diff, and the result is silently wrong. Check out full history in CI ( fetch-depth: 0 ), or the merge base won't resolve. The library API is not stable at 0.x. The command line is the better-supported interface."},{"id":"get-started","heading":"Get started","text":"Install. cargo install patchcov --locked , cargo binstall patchcov , or a prebuilt binary for Linux, macOS or Windows from the GitHub releases. Run it on your branch. Produce a per-line report from your test run, then patchcov diff --report head.lcov . Add the gate. Add action-works/patchcov-action@v2 to your pull-request workflow with fail-under-patch , and let the comment and the exit code do the rest. Command line & Rust library rust-works/patchcov Install it, run patchcov diff , read the docs GitHub Action action-works/patchcov-action Comment and gate on every pull request"},{"id":"further-reading","heading":"Further reading","text":"rust-works/patchcov : the command, its usage guide, the reference for every flag and the JSON schema. Explanation : why patchcov's total differs from llvm-cov's summary, and how scoping and tolerate masking work. action-works/patchcov-action : fat and thin modes, sharded runs, merge queues and every input. docs.rs/patchcov : the analysis as a Rust library."}]},{"href":"docs/component-gallery.html","title":"Component gallery","kind":"reference","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Reference Component gallery Every building block in the theme, rendered live. View source to copy the markup — each section is self-contained. Pages are plain HTML linking theme/bh.css and theme/bh.js — no build step. Chrome (top bar, contents, progress, anchors, theme toggle) is added automatically. Interactive parts are web components that fall back to readable static content."},{"id":"typography","heading":"Typography","text":"A .lead paragraph opens a section with a little more presence. Body copy is set in IBM Plex Sans at a comfortable measure, with headings in Fraunces. Inline elements: inline_code() , ⌘ K , highlighted text , ADR , and a link . Sidenotes keep asides out of the main line of thought. On wide screens this floats into the margin; on narrow screens tap the number to expand it."},{"id":"headings","heading":"A third-level heading","text":"Second-level headings get an automatic §n eyebrow and appear in the contents; third-level headings are indented beneath them."},{"id":"callouts","heading":"Callouts","text":"Note Neutral information the reader should not miss. Tip A shortcut or recommended practice. Gotcha Something that surprises people. Danger Data loss, security, or outage risk."},{"id":"badges","heading":"Badges","text":"Draft Proposed Accepted In review Rejected Superseded High Medium Low v2.4.0"},{"id":"cards-metrics","heading":"Cards & metrics","text":"1 Single source Tokens in one file drive every colour, size and duration. 2 Both themes Light and dark from light-dark() — no duplicated rules. 3 Degrades well No JavaScript? Steps become a list, tabs become sections. 99.95% Availability (30d) 142 ms p99 latency +18% 3.2k Requests / s +6% 0 Open incidents"},{"id":"charts","heading":"Charts","text":"Write the numbers as a table inside <bh-chart> to get a themed line or column chart. Hover, or focus the chart and use the arrow keys, to read every series at a point. The table stays one click away under “Show data”. Day Before After Mon 412 402 Tue 398 260 Wed 430 190 Thu 405 176 Fri 441 181 Sat 390 172 Sun 420 168 Tail latency fell by more than half once the edge limiter shed abusive bursts. emphasis puts the story in colour and the context in grey. Region Per service At the edge us-east 4.1 1.2 eu-west 3.6 1.1 ap-south 6.8 1.9 sa-east 2.2 0.8 Column charts group series per category. Only the extreme value is labelled; the axis, tooltip and table carry the rest."},{"id":"comparisons","heading":"Comparisons & decisions","text":"PostgreSQL Relational, mature, already operated by the team. Transactions across tables Existing runbooks Vertical scaling ceiling DynamoDB Managed key-value with predictable latency. No capacity planning Access patterns fixed up-front Vendor lock-in We will use PostgreSQL with read replicas, because transactional integrity outweighs elastic scaling at our current size. Revisit when write volume exceeds 5k/s. When the choice depends on how much each criterion matters, show the scoring with <bh-matrix> . Readers can move the weights and watch whether the leader changes. Scores 1–5, higher is better. Weights 0–5 say how much each criterion matters. Criterion Weight PostgreSQL DynamoDB CockroachDB Multi-table transactions 4 5 2 5 Team familiarity 3 5 2 2 Elastic scaling 2 3 5 4 Cost at current scale 3 4 3 2 Vendor independence 2 5 1 4"},{"id":"sequences","heading":"Steps & timelines","text":"Drain the node. Stop routing new traffic and wait for in-flight requests. Upgrade. Apply the new image and restart the service. Verify & restore. Health checks pass, then re-add to the pool. 14:02 Deploy begins Config change rolled to 5% of hosts. 14:06 Error rate spikes 5xx climbs to 12% on canary hosts. 14:09 Alert fires On-call paged by SLO burn alert. 14:15 Rolled back Error rate returns to baseline."},{"id":"data","heading":"Key-values & tables","text":"Service billing-api Owner Payments platform Runtime Rust 1.90, Tokio, Axum Latency by region, last 7 days. Region p50 p99 Meets SLO us-east-1 21 ms 96 ms Yes eu-west-1 24 ms 131 ms Yes ap-southeast-2 38 ms 412 ms No"},{"id":"disclosure","heading":"Disclosure & predict-then-reveal","text":"Go deeper: why not just retry? Retries multiply load during an outage. Without jittered backoff and a retry budget, a brief blip becomes a sustained overload. If each of 3 layers retries 3 times, how many calls can one request cause? 4³ = 64. Each layer makes up to 4 attempts (1 + 3 retries), and they multiply."},{"id":"code","heading":"Code","text":"Language classes get syntax highlighting (Prism, loaded only when needed). Every block gets a copy button; data-title labels it. pub fn try_acquire(&mut self, now: Instant) -> bool { self.refill(now); // ① if self.tokens >= 1.0 { self.tokens -= 1.0; // ② true } else { false // ③ } }"},{"id":"annotated-code","heading":"Annotated code","text":"let tokens = min(capacity, tokens + elapsed * rate); 1 if tokens >= cost { tokens -= cost; allow() } 2 else { reject(retry_after(cost - tokens)) } 3 Refill lazily from elapsed time — no background timer needed. Spend tokens only when the request is admitted. Tell the client exactly when to come back."},{"id":"diffs","heading":"Diffs","text":"timeout_ms: 2000 - retries: 5 + retries: 2 + retry_budget: 0.1 backoff: exponential"},{"id":"code-walkthrough","heading":"Code walkthrough","text":"For explaining an implementation, <bh-codewalk> pins the code beside the prose. As you scroll, each step highlights its lines and shades the rest. use std::time::{Duration, Instant}; pub struct TokenBucket { capacity: f64, // b: the largest burst rate: f64, // r: tokens per second tokens: f64, last: Instant, // monotonic clock } impl TokenBucket { pub fn try_acquire(&mut self, cost: f64) -> Result<(), Duration> { let now = Instant::now(); let elapsed = now.duration_since(self.last).as_secs_f64(); self.tokens = (self.tokens + elapsed * self.rate).min(self.capacity); self.last = now; if self.tokens >= cost { self.tokens -= cost; Ok(()) } else { Err(Duration::from_secs_f64((cost - self.tokens) / self.rate)) } } } The whole state is four fields. Capacity and rate are configuration; tokens and last are all the limiter remembers per client. Refill lazily. No timer runs in the background: on each call, add the tokens earned since last and clamp to capacity. Admit by spending tokens. Weighted requests just pass a larger cost . Reject with a time. The error carries exactly how long until enough tokens exist, which becomes the Retry-After header."},{"id":"diagrams","heading":"Diagrams","text":"Inline SVG drawn with theme classes — .node.c1…c6 , .edge , .zone , .label . Colours follow the theme; arrowheads are automatic. Pair a diagram with <bh-stepper for=\"…\"> to walk through it. Each step is linkable ( #gallery-arch-step-3 ), and the link button in the stepper bar copies a link to the current step. A request path. Use the stepper below; edges animate while lit. External Network edge Our service Storage Infrastructure Prose can point into a diagram. Hover over the gateway , or over the service and its two stores , to light those parts. Click one to bring the diagram into view. Mark up the phrase with data-ref=\"figure-id:part-id …\" . The client calls the gateway over HTTPS; TLS terminates here. The gateway authenticates and forwards to the service . The service checks the cache first. On a miss it reads the database and back-fills the cache. Side effects are published to the queue asynchronously."},{"id":"current-vs-proposed","heading":"Current vs proposed","text":"Wrap two versions of a diagram in <bh-versions> to switch between them, or press Compare and drag the divider. Mark parts with data-change=\"added|changed|removed\" to show what the proposal changes. Every service enforces its own limit, so a client's real limit grows with the number of services. One limiter at the gateway sees all of a client's traffic, so the limit holds however many services there are."},{"id":"sequence-diagrams","heading":"Sequence diagrams","text":"Describe actors and messages, and <bh-sequence> lays them out with the theme's vocabulary. Replies are dashed, notes sit over one or more lifelines, and every part keeps its data-id , so a stepper can walk through the exchange. Client Limiter Service GET /orders refill tokens take 1 token GET /orders 200 OK later: bucket empty GET /orders 429 · Retry-After: 0.5 The limiter answers rejected requests itself; the service never sees them. The client calls the limiter. The limiter refills lazily, then takes a token. Admitted: the request reaches the service, which replies. When the bucket is empty, the limiter replies 429 without bothering the service."},{"id":"mermaid","heading":"Mermaid (fallback)","text":"For quick sequence or state diagrams, a <pre class=\"mermaid\"> renders with theme colours (loaded from a CDN only when present). Prefer hand-placed SVG for anything central to the argument. sequenceDiagram participant C as Client participant L as Limiter participant S as Service C->>L: request alt token available L->>S: forward S-->>C: 200 OK else bucket empty L-->>C: 429 Retry-After end"},{"id":"tabs","heading":"Tabs","text":"let resp = client.get(url).send().await?; resp, err := http.Get(url) resp = httpx.get(url)"},{"id":"playground","heading":"Playground","text":"Named inputs feed data-expr outputs and data-bind attributes — live models with no custom script. Replicas Per-replica capacity (rps) Offered load (rps) Utilisation % · headroom rps"},{"id":"glossary-terms","heading":"Glossary terms","text":"Words defined in the glossary are underlined automatically the first time they appear on a page. Hover over one to see its definition. For example, a gateway might enforce a rate limit with a token bucket and answer rejected calls with Retry-After, keeping p99 latency inside the SLO. The popup stays put while it's open. Move the pointer into it to select and copy text or follow its links. Click a term, or press Enter on it, to pin the popup; Esc or a click elsewhere closes it. To mark a word explicitly, use data-term=\"id\" , as in this GCRA reference . Add class=\"no-glossary\" to keep a passage unmarked."},{"id":"layout","heading":"Layout","text":"Children of .bh-article sit in the reading column. Add .wide to use the gutters, or .full to go edge to edge. .wide — figures, tables, comparisons .full — hero visuals, big diagrams"}]},{"href":"docs/edge-rate-limiting-design.html","title":"Moving rate limiting to the edge","kind":"design","example":true,"sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Design document Moving rate limiting to the edge Replace per-service limiters with one token bucket per client in the gateway, backed by Redis, so a client's limit holds however far we scale. Problem: per-service limits multiply with services and replicas. One client sent 9× its contract without being limited. Proposal: enforce limits once, in the gateway, with an atomic Redis script. Services keep only a coarse safety limit. Ask: review the fail-open policy and the rollout by 17 October."},{"id":"context","heading":"Context & problem","text":"Since ADR-001 , each service has run its own token bucket. That shipped quickly, but a limit enforced in nine places isn't a limit. During the September incident, one client's traffic spread across three services with three replicas each, and every replica admitted it. 9× Peak traffic vs contract, never limited 3 × 3 Services × replicas the limit was split across 412 ms p99 for everyone else during the incident"},{"id":"goals","heading":"Goals & non-goals","text":"Goals One limit per client, accurate to within 5% Rejected requests never reach a service Under 2 ms added at p99 Non-goals DDoS protection: that stays with the CDN Per-endpoint quotas (a later phase)"},{"id":"design","heading":"Proposed design","text":"Compare the two versions below. Today the limiters sit inside each service. In the proposal, the gateway checks one bucket per client in Redis before routing to any service . Each service limits on its own, so a client's real limit is the per-service limit × replicas × services. One bucket per client, checked once at the gateway: the limit holds however many services and replicas there are."},{"id":"request-path","heading":"The request path","text":"The gateway makes one Redis round trip per request. The script refills and takes tokens atomically, so concurrent requests can never spend the same token twice. Client Gateway Redis Orders GET /orders EVALSHA bucket refill + take, atomically allowed · 7.4 left GET /orders 200 OK a burst empties the bucket GET /orders EVALSHA bucket denied · retry 0.4 s 429 · Retry-After: 1 The service only ever sees admitted requests; Redis answers in well under a millisecond in-region. A request reaches the gateway, keyed by its API key. The gateway runs the bucket script in Redis. Refill and take happen in one atomic step. Admitted requests are routed on as before. Once a burst empties the bucket, the gateway answers 429 itself, with a Retry-After the client can trust."},{"id":"limiter-script","heading":"The limiter script","text":"-- KEYS[1] = bucket key (one per API key) -- ARGV = capacity, rate (tokens/s), now (ms, from Redis TIME), cost local b, r, now, cost = tonumber(ARGV[1]), tonumber(ARGV[2]), tonumber(ARGV[3]), tonumber(ARGV[4]) local s = redis.call('HMGET', KEYS[1], 'tokens', 'ts') local tokens = tonumber(s[1]) or b local ts = tonumber(s[2]) or now tokens = math.min(b, tokens + math.max(0, now - ts) / 1000 * r) local ok = tokens >= cost if ok then tokens = tokens - cost end redis.call('HSET', KEYS[1], 'tokens', tokens, 'ts', now) redis.call('PEXPIRE', KEYS[1], math.ceil(b / r * 1000) + 1000) return { ok and 1 or 0, tostring(tokens) } Inputs. The gateway passes capacity and rate from the client's plan, plus the current time from Redis itself, so gateway clocks never matter. Read the bucket. A client seen for the first time starts with a full bucket. Refill, then take. This is the same arithmetic as the in-process version. Running it as a script makes the read-modify-write atomic. Write back and expire. An idle key expires once it would be full anyway, so memory tracks active clients only. Answer. Admitted or not, plus the tokens left, which the gateway turns into Retry-After ."},{"id":"alternatives","heading":"Alternatives considered","text":"The gateway option wins under the weights we agreed in the kickoff. Move the weights to test how sensitive the choice is: favouring time to ship above accuracy brings back per-service limits. Scores 1–5, higher is better. Weights 0–5 say how much each criterion matters. Criterion Weight Per service Gateway + Redis CDN rules Accuracy of the limit 5 1 5 3 Latency added 3 5 3 4 Operational cost 3 4 3 4 Sees API keys and plans 4 5 5 1 Time to ship 2 5 3 4"},{"id":"results","heading":"Canary results","text":"We ran the gateway limiter for the noisiest 5% of clients for a week. Everyone else's tail latency stopped tracking those clients' bursts. Day Per-service limits Edge limiter Mon 380 371 Tue 412 214 Wed 395 188 Thu 441 181 Fri 404 179 The canary started on Tuesday. p99 for the other 95% of clients fell by more than half and stayed flat through the noisy clients' bursts."},{"id":"risks","heading":"Risks & mitigations","text":"Risk Likelihood Impact Mitigation Redis unavailable Low High Fail open after 5 ms; services' coarse safety limits still apply Hot key for one huge client Medium Medium Shard that client's bucket by region; reconcile every second Clock skew between gateways Low Low None needed: the script reads time from Redis"},{"id":"rollout","heading":"Rollout plan","text":"Week 1 Canary: noisiest 5% of clients Done. The results are above. Week 2 All clients, shadow mode Decide but don't reject; compare against per-service decisions. Week 3 Enforce at the edge Exit criterion: under 0.1% disagreement in shadow mode. Week 5 Retire per-service limiters Keep a coarse safety limit at 10× the plan rate."},{"id":"decision","heading":"Decision","text":"We will enforce client rate limits at the gateway with the Redis bucket script, failing open, and retire the per-service limiters. Recorded as ADR-002 , which supersedes ADR-001."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html","title":"Glossary","kind":"reference","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Reference Glossary Shared definitions for terms used across these documents. Defined words are underlined wherever they appear. Hover over one, or tap it, to read its definition without leaving the page. ADR An architecture decision record: a short, permanent note of one significant decision, covering its context, the options considered and the consequences. ADRs are superseded by new records rather than edited. Back-reference In LZ77 compression, an instruction to copy L bytes starting D bytes back in the output already produced, instead of storing them again. DEFLATE allows distances up to 32,768 bytes. Step by step Backoff Waiting longer after each failed attempt before retrying, usually doubling the delay each time. Without jitter , clients that failed together retry together. Barrier A GPU instruction that makes every thread in a threadgroup wait until all of them arrive, and makes their earlier memory writes visible to each other. There is no safe barrier across threadgroups. Burst A short spike of requests above the average rate. In a token bucket, the largest burst allowed is the bucket's capacity b . Cache miss A lookup that finds nothing in the cache, so the request falls through to the slower source of truth and usually back-fills the cache. Canonical Huffman code A Huffman code rebuilt from code lengths alone: symbols are sorted by length, then by value, and each gets the previous code plus one, shifted left when the length grows. A DEFLATE block sends only the lengths. Step by step Compute kernel A function that runs on the GPU once per thread of a dispatch . Each thread reads its own index to decide which part of the work it does. CRC-32 A 32-bit checksum computed by polynomial division over the bits of the data. ZIP and gzip store one per file so a decoder can detect corruption. Checksums of adjacent pieces can be combined, which allows computing it in parallel. Cyclic redundancy check (Wikipedia) DEFLATE The compression format inside ZIP, gzip and PNG: LZ77 back-references plus Huffman coding , organised in blocks. Its bit stream has no index, so decoding is serial. Step by step · RFC 1951 Dispatch One launch of a compute kernel over a grid of threads, divided into threadgroups . Dispatches in one command buffer run in order, which is the safe way to order work between threadgroups. Divergence When lanes of one SIMD group take different sides of a branch. The hardware runs each side in turn with the other lanes switched off, so the branch costs the sum of both sides. End-of-block Symbol 256 of DEFLATE 's literal/length alphabet. It closes every Huffman-coded block, so it needs a code of its own even though it appears once. Step by step GCRA The generic cell rate algorithm. It is equivalent to a token bucket, but stores one timestamp per key: the theoretical arrival time of the next conforming request. Generic cell rate algorithm (Wikipedia) Huffman code A variable-length binary code that gives frequent symbols short bit patterns and rare ones long patterns. No code is a prefix of another, so a decoder can read them back to back without separators. Step by step · Huffman coding (Wikipedia) Jitter Randomness added to retry or polling delays, so many clients that start together don't keep acting in lock-step and overload a recovering service. Lane One thread's slot within a SIMD group . Each lane has its own registers, but all lanes execute the same instruction at the same time. lcov A plain-text coverage format that lists, per source file, how many times each line ran. Most coverage tools can write it, which makes it the common currency between them and tools like patchcov. Leaky bucket A queue that drains at a fixed rate. It smooths output to a constant pace instead of allowing bursts; compare with a token bucket . LZ77 The compression idea, from Lempel and Ziv in 1977, of replacing repeated data with back-references to an earlier occurrence. Step by step · LZ77 and LZ78 (Wikipedia) Marker In rapidgzip-style dec"}]},{"href":"docs/how-deflate-works.html","title":"How DEFLATE works","kind":"explainer","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Explainer · Compression How DEFLATE works The format inside gzip, zlib, PNG and ZIP is two simple ideas stacked on top of each other. First remove the repetition, then remove the skew. This page builds each one up, step by step, from a single 35-byte string. DEFLATE makes two separate savings. LZ77 replaces each repeat with a short (length, distance) pointer back to an earlier copy. Huffman coding then gives common symbols short bit patterns and rare ones long patterns. It matters because nearly everything uses it: gzip and zlib, PNG images, ZIP files, and Content-Encoding: gzip on the web. The common misconception is that the Huffman tree travels with the data. It doesn't. Only the code lengths are sent, and the codes are rebuilt from them by counting."},{"id":"question","heading":"A question to start","text":"How do you make abracadabra abracadabra abracadabra smaller without losing a single letter? Stored as plain bytes it takes 35 bytes, or 280 bits. Two things about it are wasteful. The same 11 letters appear three times, and the letter a makes up almost half of the text while c and d appear once per word. A good compressor should exploit both. Guess before you read on: compressed as a gzip file by zlib at its best setting (level 9), how many bytes do these 35 take? 33 bytes. That looks like almost no saving, but 18 of those bytes are the gzip wrapper: a 10-byte header, then a CRC-32 and the original length (8 bytes) at the end. The DEFLATE stream inside is 15 bytes , 43% of the input. By the end of this page you will have taken it apart bit by bit, and seen an encoder that does it in 13."},{"id":"two-ideas","heading":"Two ideas, stacked","text":"DEFLATE runs two stages, and each removes a different kind of waste. LZ77 turns the bytes into a shorter list of tokens : a literal byte, or an instruction to copy earlier bytes. Huffman coding then writes each token with as few bits as its frequency allows. literal byte back-reference (length, distance) Huffman code output bits Notice that the two savings are independent. The token count falls because of repetition (35 bytes, 10 tokens); the bits per token fall because of skew. These four colours keep the same meaning in every figure below. The two stages are independent, and each is useful alone. A file of random English words has little exact repetition but plenty of skew. A file of one byte repeated is all repetition. DEFLATE needs both because real data has both."},{"id":"lz77","heading":"LZ77: say it once, then point back","text":"Read the input left to right. Keep everything already read in a sliding window (DEFLATE's holds the last 32,768 bytes). At each position, look for the longest run of upcoming bytes that already appears in the window. If it is at least 3 bytes long, emit a back-reference : copy length bytes, starting distance bytes back. Otherwise emit the byte itself as a literal. Watch the second copy : it starts 12 bytes back but is 23 bytes long, so it reads bytes that the copy itself is still writing. Start with nothing. The cursor is at the first byte and the window behind it is empty, so there is nothing to point back to. Seven literals. None of a b r a c a d starts a run of 3 bytes seen before, so each one drops out of the input as a literal token. The window grows behind the cursor. A match. The next four bytes, abra , already appear 7 bytes back. Any run of 3 or more is worth replacing, and this one is 4 long. Emit (4, 7). The four bytes collapse into one token: copy 4 bytes from 7 back . Four letters cost one token. The space is new , so it goes out as a literal. A long match that overlaps. Everything that is left, 23 bytes, repeats what started 12 bytes back. Only 12 bytes lie between the source and the cursor, so the copy runs into its own output. Emit (23, 12). The decoder copies one byte at a time, so by the time it needs byte 13 of the copy, it has already written it. 35 bytes are now 10 tokens. Overlap is not a corner case. It is how LZ77 encodes runs: aaaaaaaa is the literal a followed by (7, 1) , \"copy 7 bytes from 1 back\"."},{"id":"huffman","heading":"Huffman: short codes for common symbols","text":"Eight bits per letter is fair only if every letter is equally likely. In abracadabra the counts are a×5, b×2, r×2, c×1 and d×1, so a should get the shortest code. Huffman's algorithm finds the best codes for known counts, and it is greedy: repeatedly join the two rarest things. Here the symbols are the letters themselves, to keep the tree small. DEFLATE runs the same algorithm on the output of LZ77 instead, as the join below shows: on counts of literals, lengths and distances, not of raw bytes. Notice that a sits one step from the root and gets a 1-bit code, while the four rare letters share the bottom row with 3-bit codes. No code is the start of another, because every letter is a leaf. Count each letter. At 8 bits a letter, the 11 letters of abracadabra cost 88 bits. Line them up, rarest first. Each letter is now a leaf, weighted by its count. Join the two rarest: c and d, 1 each, become a node of weight 2. Again. Three things now weigh 2: b, r and the new node. Join any two. Here b and r make 4. Again: 2 and 4 make 6. Only a (5) and this node are left. The last join makes the root, 11, which is every letter. The common a joined last, so it ends up nearest the root. Read the codes. Label every left branch 0 and every right branch 1. A letter's code is the path from the root to it. The total falls from 88 bits to 23. At the second join, three nodes tied at weight 2. Join a different pair and you get a different tree. Does the total change? No: it is still 23 bits. Join c+d with b instead, and the code lengths become a 1, r 2, b 3, c 4 and d 4. The tree is different, but 5×1 + 2×2 + 2×3 + 1×4 + 1×4 = 23 as well. Huffman's algorithm gives an optimal code, not the optimal code. That ambiguity is exactly what DEFLATE removes next."},{"id":"canonical","heading":"Send lengths, not trees","text":"The decoder needs the same codes as the encoder. Sending the tree would cost bits, and as the question above shows, many trees are equally good. DEFLATE fixes one rule for turning code lengths into codes, called the canonical Huffman code , so a block only has to send one small number per symbol. Notice that the codes come from the order alone. Once the letters are sorted by length and then alphabetically, each code is the previous one plus one, and gains a 0 on the right for each extra bit of length. Start from the tree. It gave a the code 0 and the others 100, 101, 110 and 111, in the order of its leaves. Throw the codes away. Keep only each letter's length. This is all a DEFLATE block sends. Sort by length, and alphabetically within a length. The first code is all zeros. a gets 0 . Count up, then shift. Add one (1), then append a 0 for each bit of extra length: b gets 100 . Count up within a length. c, d and r get 101 , 110 and 111 . The decoder runs the same rules on the same lengths, so it gets the same codes without ever seeing a tree. These are not the tree's codes, but they have the same lengths, so they cost the same 23 bits. Counting up keeps the code prefix-free: every 3-bit code starts with 1, and the only 1-bit code is 0."},{"id":"joining","heading":"How the two ideas join","text":"The two stages meet once per block, always in this order: LZ77 turns the block's bytes into tokens: literals and (length, distance) copies. Map every token to symbols from two alphabets, described below. This is the join. Count the symbols and build two Huffman codes from the counts: one for literals and lengths, one for distances. Write the block: its header (for a dynamic block, including the two codes), then each token's symbols as Huffman codes, each followed by its extra bits, then the end-of-block code. So Huffman coding never sees the raw bytes, or the tokens themselves. It counts and codes the symbols LZ77's output maps to, while the extra bits travel alongside, uncoded. The join is therefore a choice of alphabet for everything LZ77 can produce. The obvious alphabet would give every possible value its own symbol: b0 – b255 for the bytes, eob , w3 – w258 for the lengths and d1 – d32768 for the distances, 33,281 symbols in one code. DEFLATE does not do that. Its alphabets are drawn below, all 316 symbols. Pick something LZ77 can produce and see which symbol it becomes. LZ77 gives a byte end of block a length a distance Value 23 Not DEFLATE: one symbol per value w23 one of 33,281 symbols DEFLATE 270 + extra bits 0 Symbol 270 of the literal/length alphabet stands for lengths 23&#8288;–&#8288;26. 2 extra bits after it give the offset: 23 + 0 = 23. Literal/length alphabet : 286 symbols, one Huffman code bytes 0–255 256 257 258 259 260 261 262 263 264 265 266 267 268 269 270 271 272 273 274 275 276 277 278 279 280 281 282 283 284 285 Distance alphabet : 30 symbols, its own Huffman code d0 d1 d2 d3 d4 d5 d6 d7 d8 d9 d10 d11 d12 d13 d14 d15 d16 d17 d18 d19 d20 d21 d22 d23 d24 d25 d26 d27 d28 d29 Symbol 270 covers 4 lengths. The extra bits say which one: 23 +0 24 +1 25 +2 26 +3 The two changes, one at a time: A length or distance is a range symbol plus extra bits. Length 23 is not a symbol of its own. It is symbol 270, which means \"a length from 23 to 26\", followed by 2 plain extra bits, 00 , that pick 23. The extra bits are written as they are, not Huffman-coded. Short lengths (3–10) and distances (1–4) still get exact symbols, because they are the most common. Distances are coded separately. The bytes, the end of block and the length ranges share the first alphabet, so one code tells the decoder whether a literal or a copy comes next. A distance always follows a length, so the decoder always knows to read it with the second code."},{"id":"join-example","heading":"The join, token by token","text":"Here is the mapping applied to the ten tokens from the window figure , and then the codes that turn the symbols into bits. Notice that the middle row is all Huffman coding ever sees. A back-reference becomes two symbols from two alphabets (square: literal/length; rounded: distance), each followed by plain extra bits, and the end-of-block symbol costs bits too. Start from LZ77's output: eight literals and two back-references. A literal is its own symbol. The byte a (97) is symbol 97 of the literal/length alphabet , which runs from 0 to 285. All eight literals map this way. A back-reference becomes two symbols. Length 4 is symbol 258 of the same alphabet. Distance 7 is code d5 of a second, distance alphabet ; d5 covers distances 7–8, so 1 extra bit follows to pick 7. (23, 12) becomes 270, covering lengths 23–26, with 2 extra bits, then d6, covering 9–12, with 2 more. The block must end. Symbol 256, end-of-block, comes from the literal/length alphabet, so it sits among the literals and lengths and gets a code like them. Huffman codes turn symbols into bits. Each symbol is replaced by its code from its own alphabet's table. This block uses the fixed tables: 8 bits per literal, 7 for symbols 256–279, 5 per distance code. Extra bits go out as they are, uncoded. With the 3 header bits that is 103 bits, the number in the first figure."},{"id":"two-alphabets","heading":"Every range, and the decoder's loop","text":"The full mapping, for when you need the exact ranges: How LZ77's output maps onto DEFLATE's two alphabets. Where a symbol covers a range, plain extra bits follow it to pick the exact value; they are not Huffman-coded. LZ77 gives Symbol Alphabet Extra bits In the example a literal byte, 0–255 0–255, the byte's own value literal/length 0 a (byte 97) → 97 the end of the block 256 literal/length 0 256 , once at the end a length, 3–10 257–264, one each literal/length 0 length 4 → 258 a length, 11–257 265–284, each covering 2–32 lengths literal/length 1–5 length 23 → 270 ( 23–26 ) + 2 bits a length of 258 285 literal/length 0 a distance, 1–4 d0–d3, one each distance 0 a distance, 5–32,768 d4–d29, each covering 2–8,192 distances distance 1–13 distance 7 → d5 ( 7–8 ) + 1 bit; 12 → d6 ( 9–12 ) + 2 bits The decoder's inner loop runs the join backwards. Its two tables are the two alphabets: while True: sym = read_code(lit_len_table) # literal, length or end if sym < 256: out.append(sym) # a literal byte elif sym == 256: break # end of block else: i = sym - 257 length = LENGTH_BASE[i] + read_bits(LENGTH_EXTRA[i]) d = read_code(distance_table) # always follows a length distance = DIST_BASE[d] + read_bits(DIST_EXTRA[d]) for _ in range(length): # byte by byte: may overlap out.append(out[-distance])"},{"id":"block-types","heading":"Three kinds of block","text":"A stream is a series of blocks. Each block starts with 3 header bits: BFINAL (1 if this is the last block) and the 2-bit BTYPE , which says where the codes come from. Two bits allow four values, and only three are used: The values of BTYPE . Header fields are read lowest bit first, so the bits arrive in the opposite order to how the value is written. BTYPE Arrives as Block 00 0 , then 0 stored: raw bytes, no compression 01 1 , then 0 fixed Huffman codes, set by the RFC 10 0 , then 1 dynamic Huffman codes, sent at the start of the block 11 1 , then 1 reserved: invalid, and a decoder must reject the stream (zlib reports \"invalid block type\") Stored (00) No compression: up to 65,535 raw bytes after a 4-byte length and its complement. Caps the cost of incompressible data at 5 bytes per 64 KiB No saving at all Fixed Huffman (01) Codes set by the RFC: 8 or 9 bits for a literal, 7 or 8 for a length, 5 for a distance. No table to send Wins on short inputs Ignores this data's skew Dynamic Huffman (10) Codes built for this block's own counts, sent as code lengths at the start of the block. Removes the skew too Usually wins once a block holds more than a few dozen bytes of text The table costs tens of bytes Any input can be written as any of the three, and every one is valid: a decoder accepts whichever it gets. Which one wins is a sum, not a rule. The encoder works out the exact size of each kind for every block (zlib ends a block after about 16,000 symbols) and writes the smallest. Tiny blocks come out fixed because a code table costs more than it saves: for the paragraph in the chart, zlib 1.2.12 switches from fixed to dynamic once the text is 74 bytes long. Go deeper: why are the fixed codes 7–9 bits long? A prefix code has a budget. A code of L bits uses up 2 − L of all possible bit patterns, since nothing else may start with it, and the shares can't add up to more than 1. The fixed code must cover 288 symbols without knowing which are common, and spread evenly that is about log 2 288 ≈ 8.2 bits each. The RFC spends the budget exactly: Symbols Count Bits Share of the budget bytes 0–143 (all of ASCII) 144 8 144 / 256 = 0.5625 bytes 144–255 112 9 112 / 512 = 0.21875 256–279: end of block and short lengths 24 7 24 / 128 = 0.1875 280–287: long lengths (286 and 287 unused) 8 8 8 / 256 = 0.03125 total 288 1 The 7-bit codes for end-of-block and common lengths are paid for by making the upper half of the byte values, rare in text, cost 9 bits. Only a code fitted to the data can make a common symbol short, by making rare ones longer: that is what a dynamic block's code does. Input Stored Fixed Dynamic zlib -9 abracadabra ×3 (35 B) 114.3 37.1 74.3 42.9 A paragraph (381 B) 101.3 78.2 62.2 61.7 Random bytes (1 KiB) 100.5 105.6 105.2 100.5 Each input has a different winner. The short string can't pay for a table; the paragraph can; random bytes can't be compressed at all, so storing them is cheapest. The first three columns are the same tokens written as each block type; zlib picks per block."},{"id":"dynamic-header","heading":"Compressing the code lengths","text":"A dynamic block opens with up to 316 code lengths, one per symbol of both alphabets. Sent as 4-bit numbers that would be about 160 bytes, more than many blocks save. So DEFLATE compresses the lengths too, with a third, smaller Huffman code. Count the alphabets. HLIT and HDIST , plain 5-bit numbers, say how many literal/length and distance codes follow, so unused symbols at the top cost nothing. Run-length the lengths. The sequence of lengths is written with 19 symbols: 0–15 are a length, 16 repeats the previous length 3–6 times, 17 and 18 write a run of 3–10 or 11–138 zeros. Like a length symbol, each run symbol is followed by plain extra bits that give its count: 2 bits after 16, 3 after 17 and 7 after 18. Long runs of unused byte values become one symbol. Code those symbols. The 19 symbols get their own canonical code, whose lengths come first, as plain 3-bit numbers, in the fixed order 16, 17, 18, 0, 8, 7, 9, 6, 10, 5, 11, 4, 12, 3, 13, 2, 14, 1, 15. HCLEN (4 bits) says how many are sent. The order puts the lengths that are usually unused last, so they can be left off. For the 381-byte paragraph in the chart, zlib's whole header ( HLIT = 270, HDIST = 17, HCLEN = 16, then the lengths) is 352 bits, about 44 bytes. That is the price of the table, and the paragraph is long enough to repay it."},{"id":"not-symbols","heading":"Everything in the stream that isn't a symbol","text":"A DEFLATE stream is mostly Huffman codes, but not entirely. Bits are written in two ways, and a decoder must know which kind comes next: A Huffman code is read one bit at a time, most significant bit first, because the decoder walks down the code tree as each bit arrives. Everything else is a plain number of a fixed, known width, read lowest bit first. Each byte of the stream fills from its lowest bit up, so a plain number's bits arrive in the order they are packed. Every plain field in a DEFLATE stream. None of these is Huffman-coded: the decoder knows each one's width from what came before it. Field Bits Where Holds BFINAL 1 start of every block 1 if this is the last block BTYPE 2 after BFINAL the block type ( values ) length extra bits 0–5 after a length symbol the offset within the symbol's range of lengths distance extra bits 0–13 after a distance symbol the offset within the symbol's range of distances HLIT 5 dynamic block, after BTYPE the number of literal/length code lengths, minus 257 HDIST 5 after HLIT the number of distance code lengths, minus 1 HCLEN 4 after HDIST the number of code-length-code lengths, minus 4 code-length-code lengths 3 each after HCLEN , 4–19 of them the lengths of the code that compresses the code lengths, in the fixed order run extra bits 2, 3 or 7 after code-length symbol 16, 17 or 18 the run's count, minus 3, 3 or 11 alignment 0–7 stored block, after BTYPE nothing: skipped to reach the next byte boundary LEN , NLEN 16 each stored block, after the alignment the number of bytes, then its ones' complement as a check stored bytes 8 each stored block, after NLEN the data itself, byte for byte final padding 0–7 after the last block nothing: fills the last byte Everything else in the stream is a Huffman code: the literal/length and distance symbols, and, inside a dynamic block's header, the code-length symbols. The worked example's 119 bits show both kinds side by side in the bit walk ."},{"id":"try-it","heading":"Try it","text":"Type anything, or pick an example. The tokens are what LZ77 found; the numbers show each saving on its own and both together. Every count is the size of a real DEFLATE stream that this page's encoder writes, not an estimate. Text to compress 35 bytes abracadabra abracadabra abracadabra abracadabra ×3 A paragraph One letter ×300 No repeats 280 bits as plain bytes 223 bits with Huffman alone 103 bits with LZ77 alone (fixed codes) 203 bits with both, incl. a 159 -bit table 13 bytes out, as a fixed block Write it as the smallest stored fixed dynamic 13 bytes ( 103 bits) as a fixed block: 4b 4c 2a 4a 4c 4e 4c 01 51 0a 38 d8 00 LZ77 replaced 27 of 35 bytes with 2 back-references, leaving 8 literals. Codes built for this text would need a 159-bit table, more than they save, so the fixed codes win. For comparison, your browser's own DEFLATE ( CompressionStream ) wrote … bytes. literal byte back-reference (length, distance) Click a back-reference to see the bytes it copies. a b r a c a d (4,7) ␣ (23,12) The token stream as a table # Token Stands for At byte 1 a a 0 2 b b 1 3 r r 2 4 a a 3 5 c c 4 6 a a 5 7 d d 6 8 (4,7) abra 7 9 ␣ ␣ 11 10 (23,12) abracadabra abracadabra 12 Click any back-reference to see which earlier bytes it copies, and use Write it as to see the same tokens as each kind of block: the smallest is what an encoder would send, but every one decodes. The paragraph shows both savings clearly: LZ77 finds the repeated words, and the skew of English letters pays for a dynamic table. One letter ×300 is almost all repetition. \"No repeats\" has nothing for LZ77 and too little text to pay for a table, so it comes out slightly larger than it went in."},{"id":"one-block","heading":"One real block, bit by bit","text":"These are the bits after Huffman coding: each literal, length and distance appears as its code from the fixed table, not as a raw byte or number. Here is what the reference zlib (version 1.2.12) actually wrote at level 9 for the opening string: the 15 bytes 4b 4c 2a 4a 4c 4e 4c 49 04 52 0a 89 d8 d9 00 , read the way a decoder reads them. Bits are taken from each byte lowest first. Huffman codes are read one bit at a time, most significant first; header fields and extra bits are plain numbers, lowest bit first. 1 BFINAL = 1: last block 10 BTYPE = 01: fixed codes 10010001 literal 97 'a' (byte 01100001) 10010010 literal 98 'b' 10100010 literal 114 'r' 10010001 literal 97 'a' 10010011 literal 99 'c' 10010001 literal 97 'a' 10010100 literal 100 'd' 10010001 literal 97 'a' 0000001 length symbol 257: length 3 00101 distance code 5: distances 7–8 0 + 1 extra bit = 0: distance 7 01010000 literal 32 ' ' 10010001 literal 97 'a' 0001101 length symbol 269: lengths 19–22 11 + 2 extra bits = 3: length 22 00110 distance code 6: distances 9–12 11 + 2 extra bits = 3: distance 12 0000000 end of block (symbol 256) The block header. BFINAL is 1, so this is the last block. The next two bits arrive as 1 0 , lowest first, so BTYPE is binary 01: the fixed Huffman codes (see the BTYPE values ). For 35 bytes, a dynamic table would cost more than it saves. Eight literals, 8 bits each. In the fixed code, bytes 0–143 are 00110000 plus the byte, so a (97) is 10010001 . That is no shorter than the raw byte, 01100001 : the fixed code isn't fitted to this text, so it gives every byte from 0 to 143 the same 8 bits ( why ). This block's whole saving comes from LZ77's two copies. Notice also that zlib wrote the second a of abra as a literal. A back-reference, (3, 7). Length symbol 257 means 3, with no extra bits. Distance code 5 covers 7–8, and its one extra bit, 0, picks 7. zlib copied bra where the window figure copied abra : 13 bits for 3 bytes. Two more literals: the space (32) and another a . The long, overlapping copy, (22, 12). Symbol 269 covers lengths 19–22, and the extra bits 11 add 3: length 22. Distance code 6 covers 9–12, plus 3: distance 12. 16 bits stand for 22 bytes. Only 12 bytes lie between the source and the destination, but the copy is 22 long. Watch the output : the decoder copies one byte at a time, so when it reaches byte 25 it reads byte 13, which this same copy wrote 12 steps earlier. Every byte it reads already exists by the time it needs it. End of block. Symbol 256 is seven zeros in the fixed code. That makes 119 bits; one more bit of padding fills the 15th byte. Replay the copy The decoder's output for zlib's stream. A copy always reads to the left of where it writes. Striped bytes are read by the copy that wrote them: the copy starts 12 bytes back but runs for 22, so from byte 25 on it reads its own output. Why is zlib's version bigger than the figure's? zlib wrote 119 bits; the parse in the window figure needs 103. DEFLATE fixes how to decode , not how to encode: any token stream that rebuilds the input is valid. zlib's hash chains use position 0 as their \"no match\" marker, so zlib never copies from the very first byte of a stream. Both of its copies here would have started there, so each starts one byte later, after an extra literal a : 16 bits lost. On real files, zlib's smarter match choices win by far more. Other builds choose differently again: Chromium's fork of zlib, in Chrome and in Node's official binaries, writes 17 bytes for this string."},{"id":"serial","heading":"Why decoding is serial","text":"The output is dense: there are no separators and no index. A code's length is known only once it has been decoded, so the decoder cannot find where symbol n + 1 starts without decoding symbol n . Back-references add a second chain: a copy reads bytes that earlier tokens wrote. That is why a gzip file is decompressed by one core, front to back, however many cores the machine has. Getting around it, for example to decode on a GPU, means finding where blocks start without decoding what comes before, and resolving copies whose sources aren't decoded yet. A later series here takes that on; nothing on this page depends on it."},{"id":"edge-cases","heading":"Edge cases","text":"Overlapping copies Allowed and common: distance 1 with length 258 repeats the previous byte 258 times. A decoder must copy byte by byte, not with a block copy, or it reads bytes that haven't been written yet. The longest match 258 bytes, with its own symbol (285) and no extra bits, so with the fixed codes a long run costs 13 bits per 258 bytes. 258 = 255 + 3: a length fits in a byte once the minimum of 3 is subtracted. End of block Symbol 256 ends every Huffman block, even the last. It needs a code, so it counts as a symbol when the tree is built. Empty input zlib writes 2 bytes, 03 00 : a final fixed block holding only the end-of-block code. Incompressible input Stored blocks cap the overhead at 5 bytes per 65,535. A large file of random bytes grows by under 0.01%; the 1 KiB in the chart grows by 0.5%."},{"id":"further-reading","heading":"Further reading","text":"RFC 1951, DEFLATE : the format itself, short and readable. Section 3.2 has the alphabets and the canonical code. RFC 1952, gzip and RFC 1950, zlib : the two wrappers, a few header bytes and a checksum around the same stream. Huffman (1952), \"A Method for the Construction of Minimum-Redundancy Codes\" : the original paper. Ziv and Lempel (1977), \"A Universal Algorithm for Sequential Data Compression\" : where LZ77 gets its name. zlib's algorithm.txt : how zlib's encoder chooses matches, which this page leaves out."}]},{"href":"docs/token-bucket-rate-limiting.html","title":"Token bucket rate limiting","kind":"explainer","example":true,"sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Explainer · Rate limiting Token bucket rate limiting How one small data structure lets clients burst when they've been quiet, while still holding them to an average rate — and the production details that the textbook version leaves out. A bucket holds up to b tokens and refills at r tokens per second. Each request spends a token; no token, no admission. That gives you two knobs with clear meanings: b is the largest burst, r is the sustained rate. You never need a timer. Refill is computed lazily from elapsed time whenever a request arrives."},{"id":"mental-model","heading":"The mental model","text":"Picture a bucket under a dripping tap. Each drip is a token. A request may pass only if it can take a token out. If the bucket is full, the extra drips spill away and are lost. That spill is the whole design. Saving is capped at b tokens, so a client that has been idle for an hour can't then fire an hour's worth of requests at once. The token bucket is equivalent to GCRA (the generic cell rate algorithm) from ATM networking. GCRA stores one timestamp instead of a token count and a timestamp. A client that has been idle for at least b/r seconds starts with a full bucket. The limiter is the only thing that moves. Refill isn't a background process. It is calculated when a request arrives."},{"id":"how-it-works","heading":"How a request is decided","text":"A request arrives at the limiter with a timestamp now . Refill lazily. Add (now − last) × r tokens and clamp to b . Anything over the cap spills. Store last = now . Try to take a token. If tokens ≥ 1 , subtract one. A request can cost more than one token. Weight expensive endpoints that way. Admitted. Forward the request. The state for this client is just two numbers: tokens and last . Rejected. Return 429 with Retry-After = (1 − tokens) / r . The client knows exactly how long to wait, so it doesn't need to guess with a backoff."},{"id":"simulation","heading":"Watch it run","text":"The slots show tokens in the bucket, and the partly filled slot is the next token arriving. Below them is the last ten seconds of requests. Set the background traffic, then press Burst ×10 and watch the bucket drain. Capacity b Refill rate r (tokens/s) Background traffic (req/s) Send 1 Burst ×10 Reset Tokens 0 · admitted 0 · rejected 0 · refill from empty takes s b = 10, r = 1/s, bucket full. A client sends 15 requests at once, then 1 per second. What happens? The first 10 are admitted and the next 5 are rejected. After that, every request at 1 per second is admitted. Each one spends the single token that arrived in the second before it, so the bucket stays almost empty. The client can't burst again until it slows below r and lets tokens build up."},{"id":"choosing-parameters","heading":"Choosing b and r","text":"Start from what the downstream system can take, not from what clients would like to send. r The sustained rate you can serve for every client at once, with headroom. If 200 clients share a backend that handles 10k req/s, the fair share is r ≈ 50. b The largest legitimate burst, such as a page load fanning out into parallel API calls. Too small and real users hit 429s. Too large and one client can saturate a replica briefly. cost Requests don't all weigh the same. Charge a report export 20 tokens and a health check 0."},{"id":"implementation","heading":"Implementation","text":"The core is about ten lines. The distributed version moves the same arithmetic into Redis, so the read-modify-write is atomic. use std::time::{Duration, Instant}; pub struct TokenBucket { capacity: f64, // b — largest burst rate: f64, // r — tokens per second tokens: f64, last: Instant, // monotonic clock: immune to NTP jumps } impl TokenBucket { pub fn new(capacity: f64, rate: f64) -> Self { Self { capacity, rate, tokens: capacity, last: Instant::now() } } /// Ok(()) if admitted, otherwise Err(how long until `cost` tokens exist). pub fn try_acquire(&mut self, cost: f64) -> Result<(), Duration> { let now = Instant::now(); let elapsed = now.duration_since(self.last).as_secs_f64(); self.tokens = (self.tokens + elapsed * self.rate).min(self.capacity); self.last = now; if self.tokens >= cost { self.tokens -= cost; Ok(()) } else { Err(Duration::from_secs_f64((cost - self.tokens) / self.rate)) } } } type Bucket struct { mu sync.Mutex capacity float64 // b rate float64 // r, tokens per second tokens float64 last time.Time } func (b *Bucket) TryAcquire(cost float64) (ok bool, retryAfter time.Duration) { b.mu.Lock() defer b.mu.Unlock() now := time.Now() // carries a monotonic reading; Sub() uses it b.tokens = math.Min(b.capacity, b.tokens+now.Sub(b.last).Seconds()*b.rate) b.last = now if b.tokens >= cost { b.tokens -= cost return true, 0 } return false, time.Duration((cost - b.tokens) / b.rate * float64(time.Second)) } -- KEYS[1] = bucket key -- ARGV = capacity, rate (tokens/s), now (ms, from the Redis TIME command), cost local b, r, now, cost = tonumber(ARGV[1]), tonumber(ARGV[2]), tonumber(ARGV[3]), tonumber(ARGV[4]) local s = redis.call('HMGET', KEYS[1], 'tokens', 'ts') local tokens = tonumber(s[1]) or b local ts = tonumber(s[2]) or now tokens = math.min(b, tokens + math.max(0, now - ts) / 1000 * r) local ok = tokens >= cost if ok then tokens = tokens - cost end redis.call('HSET', KEYS[1], 'tokens', tokens, 'ts', now) redis.call('PEXPIRE', KEYS[1], math.ceil(b / r * 1000) + 1000) -- idle keys vanish when full return { ok and 1 or 0, tostring(tokens) }"},{"id":"alternatives","heading":"How it compares","text":"Algorithm Bursts State per key Boundary spikes Best for Token bucket Up to b 2 numbers None APIs: tolerate bursts, enforce an average Leaky bucket (queue) Smoothed queue None Shaping output to a fixed rate Fixed window Up to 2× at edges 1 counter Yes Coarse quotas (per day) Sliding log Exact N timestamps None Low volume, exact limits Sliding window counter Approximate 2 counters Small A cheap approximation of the log"},{"id":"gotchas","heading":"Gotchas in production","text":"Wall-clock time Measure elapsed time with a monotonic clock. If NTP steps the wall clock backwards, the elapsed time comes out negative. Without a clamp, that can drain the bucket, or in some code mint a huge pile of tokens. Per-node limits multiply Ten gateway replicas, each with its own bucket at r = 100, give a client an effective limit of 1,000. Either share state (the Redis script above) or divide r by the replica count and accept some unfairness. Read-modify-write races A distributed limiter that does GET and then SET as two round trips lets concurrent requests spend the same token twice. Run the update atomically, with a Lua script or a database transaction. Go deeper: why return Retry-After? Clients that get a bare 429 back off blindly, usually with exponential backoff, and often all at the same moment. You already know when the next token will exist, so tell them. That turns a stampede of retries into an orderly queue."},{"id":"further-reading","heading":"Further reading","text":"Token bucket (Wikipedia) : the formal definition and its relation to the leaky bucket. Generic cell rate algorithm : the same idea, storing a single timestamp. RFC 9110 §10.2.3, Retry-After : the header semantics."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#adr","title":"ADR","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"ADRs, architecture decision record, architecture decision records","text":"An architecture decision record: a short, permanent note of one significant decision, covering its context, the options considered and the consequences. ADRs are superseded by new records rather than edited."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#back-reference","title":"Back-reference","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"back-references, backreference, backreferences","text":"In LZ77 compression, an instruction to copy L bytes starting D bytes back in the output already produced, instead of storing them again. DEFLATE allows distances up to 32,768 bytes. Step by step"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#backoff","title":"Backoff","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"exponential backoff","text":"Waiting longer after each failed attempt before retrying, usually doubling the delay each time. Without jitter , clients that failed together retry together."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#barrier","title":"Barrier","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"barriers","text":"A GPU instruction that makes every thread in a threadgroup wait until all of them arrive, and makes their earlier memory writes visible to each other. There is no safe barrier across threadgroups."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#burst","title":"Burst","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"bursts","text":"A short spike of requests above the average rate. In a token bucket, the largest burst allowed is the bucket's capacity b ."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#cache-miss","title":"Cache miss","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"cache misses","text":"A lookup that finds nothing in the cache, so the request falls through to the slower source of truth and usually back-fills the cache."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#canonical-huffman-code","title":"Canonical Huffman code","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"canonical Huffman codes, canonical code, canonical codes","text":"A Huffman code rebuilt from code lengths alone: symbols are sorted by length, then by value, and each gets the previous code plus one, shifted left when the length grows. A DEFLATE block sends only the lengths. Step by step"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#compute-kernel","title":"Compute kernel","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"compute kernels, kernel, kernels, compute shader, compute shaders","text":"A function that runs on the GPU once per thread of a dispatch . Each thread reads its own index to decide which part of the work it does."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#crc-32","title":"CRC-32","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"CRC, CRCs, CRC32","text":"A 32-bit checksum computed by polynomial division over the bits of the data. ZIP and gzip store one per file so a decoder can detect corruption. Checksums of adjacent pieces can be combined, which allows computing it in parallel. Cyclic redundancy check (Wikipedia)"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#deflate","title":"DEFLATE","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"The compression format inside ZIP, gzip and PNG: LZ77 back-references plus Huffman coding , organised in blocks. Its bit stream has no index, so decoding is serial. Step by step · RFC 1951"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#dispatch","title":"Dispatch","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"dispatches","text":"One launch of a compute kernel over a grid of threads, divided into threadgroups . Dispatches in one command buffer run in order, which is the safe way to order work between threadgroups."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#divergence","title":"Divergence","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"divergent, diverge, diverges","text":"When lanes of one SIMD group take different sides of a branch. The hardware runs each side in turn with the other lanes switched off, so the branch costs the sum of both sides."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#end-of-block","title":"End-of-block","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"end of block, end-of-block symbol, end-of-block code","text":"Symbol 256 of DEFLATE 's literal/length alphabet. It closes every Huffman-coded block, so it needs a code of its own even though it appears once. Step by step"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#gcra","title":"GCRA","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"generic cell rate algorithm","text":"The generic cell rate algorithm. It is equivalent to a token bucket, but stores one timestamp per key: the theoretical arrival time of the next conforming request. Generic cell rate algorithm (Wikipedia)"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#huffman-code","title":"Huffman code","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"Huffman codes, Huffman coding, Huffman table, Huffman tables","text":"A variable-length binary code that gives frequent symbols short bit patterns and rare ones long patterns. No code is a prefix of another, so a decoder can read them back to back without separators. Step by step · Huffman coding (Wikipedia)"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#jitter","title":"Jitter","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"Randomness added to retry or polling delays, so many clients that start together don't keep acting in lock-step and overload a recovering service."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#lane","title":"Lane","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"lanes","text":"One thread's slot within a SIMD group . Each lane has its own registers, but all lanes execute the same instruction at the same time."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#lcov","title":"lcov","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"lcov report, lcov reports","text":"A plain-text coverage format that lists, per source file, how many times each line ran. Most coverage tools can write it, which makes it the common currency between them and tools like patchcov."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#leaky-bucket","title":"Leaky bucket","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"leaky buckets","text":"A queue that drains at a fixed rate. It smooths output to a constant pace instead of allowing bursts; compare with a token bucket ."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#lz77","title":"LZ77","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"The compression idea, from Lempel and Ziv in 1977, of replacing repeated data with back-references to an earlier occurrence. Step by step · LZ77 and LZ78 (Wikipedia)"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#marker","title":"Marker","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"markers","text":"In rapidgzip-style decoding, a 16-bit placeholder meaning \"the byte n positions before this chunk started\". It lets a decoder start mid-stream without the history, and is replaced by the real byte once the previous chunk is decoded."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#merge-base","title":"Merge base","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"merge bases, fork point, fork points","text":"The newest commit that a branch and its target (usually main ) share: where the branch forked. Comparing against it, rather than the tip of main , shows only what the branch itself changed."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#monotonic-clock","title":"Monotonic clock","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"monotonic clocks","text":"A clock that only moves forwards. Use it to measure elapsed time, because the wall clock can jump backwards when NTP corrects it."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#occupancy","title":"Occupancy","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"How many SIMD groups a GPU core keeps resident at once. More resident groups give the core more work to switch to while others wait for memory. Registers and threadgroup memory per group limit it."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#p99","title":"p99","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"99th percentile","text":"The 99th-percentile latency: 99% of requests complete at least this fast. Tail latency like this, rather than the average, is what users notice."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#patch-coverage","title":"Patch coverage","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"diff coverage","text":"The share of the executable lines a change added that the tests ran. Unlike the project-wide total, it shows the gaps in new code even when they are too small to move the total. Announcing patchcov lets you try it."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#rate-limiting","title":"Rate limiting","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"rate limit, rate limits, rate limiter, rate limiters","text":"Capping how many requests a client may make in a period of time, to protect a service and share its capacity fairly. Token bucket rate limiting explains the most common algorithm."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#read-replica","title":"Read replica","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"read replicas","text":"A copy of a database that serves reads and follows the primary asynchronously, so it can lag slightly behind."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#retry-after","title":"Retry-After","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"An HTTP response header that tells the client how long to wait before retrying, sent with 429 or 503 . RFC 9110 §10.2.3"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#retry-budget","title":"Retry budget","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"retry budgets","text":"A cap on retries as a fraction of normal traffic (for example 10%). It stops retries from multiplying load during an outage."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#shuffle","title":"Shuffle","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"shuffles, lane shuffle, lane shuffles","text":"A GPU instruction in which every lane reads a register value from another lane of its SIMD group, without going through memory. In Metal it is simd_shuffle ; in HLSL, WaveReadLaneAt ."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#simd-group","title":"SIMD group","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"SIMD groups, simdgroup, simdgroups, warp, warps, subgroup, subgroups, wavefront, wavefronts","text":"The set of threads a GPU executes in lock-step: 32 on Apple GPUs (NVIDIA calls it a warp, Vulkan a subgroup, HLSL a wave). One instruction is fetched once and executed by every lane ."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#sliding-window","title":"Sliding window","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"sliding windows","text":"In LZ77 , the most recent bytes already processed, which back-references may copy from. It moves forward with the input; DEFLATE 's is 32,768 bytes. Step by step"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#slo","title":"SLO","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"SLOs, service level objective, service level objectives","text":"A service level objective: a reliability target, such as 99.9% of requests succeeding over 30 days. It is set below 100% on purpose, leaving an error budget."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#spir-v","title":"SPIR-V","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"The Khronos binary intermediate language for GPU programs. Vulkan drivers consume it directly, and SPIRV-Cross can translate it into Metal Shading Language. SPIR-V registry"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#threadgroup","title":"Threadgroup","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"threadgroups, workgroup, workgroups, thread block, thread blocks","text":"A group of GPU threads that runs on one core and can share threadgroup memory and barriers . Vulkan calls it a workgroup and CUDA a thread block."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#threadgroup-memory","title":"Threadgroup memory","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"groupshared, groupshared memory, shared memory, workgroup memory","text":"Small, fast on-chip memory shared by the threads of one threadgroup : at most 32 KiB per threadgroup on Apple GPUs. HLSL calls it groupshared ."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#tls","title":"TLS","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"transport layer security","text":"Transport Layer Security: the protocol that encrypts and authenticates a connection, as in HTTPS. A gateway that \"terminates TLS\" decrypts traffic there."}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#token-bucket","title":"Token bucket","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"token buckets","text":"A rate limiter that refills tokens at rate r up to a capacity b , and admits each request only by spending a token. It allows bursts up to b but enforces r on average. Token bucket rate limiting"}]},{"href":"docs/glossary.html#unified-memory","title":"Unified memory","kind":"glossary","sections":[{"id":"","heading":"","text":"A design, used by Apple Silicon, in which the CPU and GPU share the same physical DRAM. A buffer can be visible to both without being copied, though first GPU use of a buffer still has a cost."}]}];
